How to Check Folder Size in Linux

Why it matters?
Imagine your server throws a warning:
     “Disk 95% full”
Now what? You need to quickly find which folder is consuming space. Here's how:

Most common command:
       du -sh /path/to/folder
This shows total size of that folder in a human-readable format.
Example:
      du -sh /var/log
This will output something like:
      120M /var/log
which means /var/log is using 120MB.


Want to find all folder sizes in current directory?
Use:
       du -sh * | sort -h
This will show size of each folder/file in the current directory, sorted by size. Great to spot the biggest consumer.


Real-World Use Case:
You're managing a prod server.
Disk usage spikes. Instead of panicking, run:
du -sh /* | sort -hr | head -10
It gives you top 10 heaviest folders under /. Fast. Clean. Critical.


Interview Tip:
If someone asks:
What’s the difference between du and df?
Say this:
du tells you how much space a folder is using
df tells you the overall disk usage (total, used, available space)
